1
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:42,572
Everybody, heads up. Keep it clear, now.

2
00:01:42,811 --> 00:01:45,091
Keep it clear.

3
00:01:45,331 --> 00:01:48,371
Stand back.

4
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:01,171
Slow it down.

5
00:02:09,771 --> 00:02:12,811
- Pushing team, move in there.
- Move in.

6
00:02:13,050 --> 00:02:17,330
- On your ready.
- I want tasers on full charge.

7
00:02:17,490 --> 00:02:20,692
Steady! Go on. Step back in.

8
00:02:20,931 --> 00:02:23,451
Ready. Move it.

9
00:02:23,691 --> 00:02:26,451
And push!

10
00:02:31,371 --> 00:02:35,531
Well, locked. Loading team, step away.

11
00:02:42,491 --> 00:02:45,932
Jophery, raise the gate.

12
00:03:01,532 --> 00:03:05,490
Block the opening! Don't let her get out!

13
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:14,450
Somebody help him!

14
00:03:14,691 --> 00:03:17,931
Work her back!

15
00:03:27,451 --> 00:03:29,972
Shoot her!

16
00:03:30,451 --> 00:03:33,372
Shoot her!

17
00:04:05,731 --> 00:04:08,771
- I heard that Hammond's not even here.
- He sends his apologies.

18
00:04:09,012 --> 00:04:11,812
There's a $20 million lawsuit
for that worker's death...

19
00:04:12,051 --> 00:04:14,372
and you're telling me
that Hammond can't see me?

20
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:17,451
He had to leave early,
his daughter's getting a divorce.

21
00:04:17,692 --> 00:04:20,451
I understand, but we need to deal
with the situation now.

22
00:04:20,692 --> 00:04:24,252
- The insurance company—
- You okay?

23
00:04:25,211 --> 00:04:29,810
The underwriters feel that the accident's
raised safety questions about the park.

24
00:04:30,052 --> 00:04:31,932
That makes the investors very anxious.

25
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:37,091
I had to promise to conduct
a thorough, on-site inspection.

26
00:04:37,132 --> 00:04:39,533
Hammond hates inspections.
They slow everything down.

27
00:04:39,773 --> 00:04:44,211
They'll pull the funding.
That'll slow him down even more.

28
00:04:48,651 --> 00:04:50,172
Watch your head.

29
00:04:50,411 --> 00:04:55,372
If two experts sign off on the island,
the insurance guys'll back off.

30
00:04:55,612 --> 00:04:58,372
I've already got lan Malcolm,
but they think he's too trendy.

31
00:04:58,612 --> 00:04:59,971
- They want Alan Grant.
- Grant?

32
00:05:00,211 --> 00:05:02,612
You'll never get him out of Montana.

33
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:05,291
Why not?

34
00:05:06,091 --> 00:05:08,610
Why not?

35
00:05:09,571 --> 00:05:12,973
Because Grant's like me.

36
00:05:13,291 --> 00:05:16,132
He's a digger.

37
00:05:57,533 --> 00:06:01,291
Dr. Grant, Dr. Sattler,
we're ready to try again.

38
00:06:01,533 --> 00:06:03,173
I hate computers.

39
00:06:03,413 --> 00:06:06,411
The feeling's mutual.

40
00:06:19,292 --> 00:06:21,932
Take a look.

41
00:06:23,252 --> 00:06:24,252
Did it work?

42
00:06:24,492 --> 00:06:27,292
- How long does this take?
- Should bring an immediate return.

43
00:06:27,531 --> 00:06:31,853
You shoot the radar into the ground,
and the bone bounces the image back.

44
00:06:32,093 --> 00:06:35,012
Bounces it back...

45
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:37,771
This new program is incredible.

46
00:06:38,012 --> 00:06:41,211
A few more years' development,
and we won't even have to dig anymore.

47
00:06:41,452 --> 00:06:43,411
Where's the fun in that?

48
00:06:43,653 --> 00:06:47,733
It's a little distorted,
but I don't think it's the computer.

49
00:06:47,971 --> 00:06:51,332
Post-mortem contraction
of the posterior neck ligaments.

50
00:06:51,572 --> 00:06:52,451
Velociraptor?

51
00:06:52,692 --> 00:06:54,332
Yes. Good shape, too.

52
00:06:54,572 --> 00:06:57,411
It's 5, 6 feet high. I'm guessing 9 feet long.

53
00:06:57,653 --> 00:06:58,771
- Look—
- What'd you do?

54
00:06:59,012 --> 00:07:00,132
He touched it.

55
00:07:00,372 --> 00:07:04,851
- Dr. Grant's not machine compatible.
- Hell, they've got it in for me.

56
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:07,451
And look at the half-moon shaped bones
in the wrist.

57
00:07:07,692 --> 00:07:11,533
It's no wonder these guys learned
how to fly.

58
00:07:12,012 --> 00:07:14,653
No, seriously.

59
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:19,812
Maybe dinosaurs have more in common
with present-day birds than with reptiles.

60
00:07:20,052 --> 00:07:23,052
Look at the pubic bone, turned backward,
like a bird's.

61
00:07:23,293 --> 00:07:27,451
Look at the vertebrae,
full of air sacs and hollows, like a bird's.

62
00:07:27,692 --> 00:07:30,451
And even the word "raptor"
means "bird of prey."

63
00:07:30,692 --> 00:07:34,052
That doesn't look very scary.

64
00:07:34,971 --> 00:07:38,292
More like a 6-foot turkey.

65
00:07:39,411 --> 00:07:41,932
A turkey?

66
00:07:42,651 --> 00:07:46,411
- Imagine yourself in the Cretaceous period.
- Here we go.

67
00:07:46,651 --> 00:07:50,533
You'd get your first look at this
6-foot turkey as you enter a clearing.

68
00:07:50,773 --> 00:07:53,653
He moves like a bird,
lightly bobbing his head.

69
00:07:53,893 --> 00:07:57,773
You keep still because maybe
his visual acuity is based on movement...

70
00:07:58,012 --> 00:08:00,533
like T. Rex and he'll lose you
if you don't move.

71
00:08:00,773 --> 00:08:03,252
But no, not Velociraptor.

72
00:08:03,492 --> 00:08:07,692
You stare at him
and he just stares right back.

73
00:08:07,932 --> 00:08:12,653
And that's when the attack comes.
Not from the front, but from the side.

74
00:08:12,893 --> 00:08:17,812
From the other two Raptors
you didn't even know were there.

75
00:08:17,853 --> 00:08:22,492
Because Velociraptor's a pack hunter.
He uses co-ordinated attack patterns...

76
00:08:22,731 --> 00:08:25,612
and he is out in force today.

77
00:08:25,851 --> 00:08:28,533
And he slashes at you with this...

78
00:08:28,773 --> 00:08:32,653
a 6-inch retractable claw,
like a razor, on the middle toe.

79
00:08:32,893 --> 00:08:36,773
He doesn't bother to bite your jugular
like a lion. No.

80
00:08:37,013 --> 00:08:39,092
He slashes at you...

81
00:08:39,332 --> 00:08:41,173
here or here...

82
00:08:41,413 --> 00:08:43,013
Alan.

83
00:08:43,253 --> 00:08:47,852
Or maybe across the belly,
spilling your intestines.

84
00:08:48,092 --> 00:08:54,212
The point is, you are alive
when they start to eat you.

85
00:08:54,412 --> 00:08:58,173
So, you know, try to show a little respect.

86
00:08:58,413 --> 00:09:00,773
Okay.

87
00:09:06,932 --> 00:09:11,173
Alan, if you wanted to scare the kid,
you could've pulled a gun on him.

88
00:09:11,413 --> 00:09:14,254
Yeah, I know. Kids.

89
00:09:14,493 --> 00:09:16,332
You want to have one of those?

90
00:09:16,573 --> 00:09:21,773
I don't want that kid,
but a breed of child could be intriguing.

91
00:09:22,013 --> 00:09:23,734
I mean, what's so wrong with kids?

92
00:09:23,972 --> 00:09:28,052
They're noisy, they're messy,
they're expensive.

93
00:09:28,293 --> 00:09:29,572
Cheap, cheap.

94
00:09:29,812 --> 00:09:31,133
They smell.

95
00:09:31,373 --> 00:09:32,692
- They don't smell!
- Some smell.

96
00:09:32,932 --> 00:09:36,613
- Give me a break!
- Babies smell.

97
00:09:42,412 --> 00:09:44,212
- Cover the site!
- Cover up the dig!

98
00:09:44,452 --> 00:09:47,014
- Tell them to shut down!
- Cut the machine!

99
00:09:47,254 --> 00:09:50,974
Cover it all up. Pull it over.

100
00:09:51,254 --> 00:09:55,094
Just cut it, will you? Shut it down!

101
00:09:55,332 --> 00:09:57,812
What?

102
00:10:10,452 --> 00:10:13,572
What the hell do you think
you're doing in here?

103
00:10:13,812 --> 00:10:16,253
We were saving that.

104
00:10:16,493 --> 00:10:20,013
For today, I guarantee it.

105
00:10:20,092 --> 00:10:22,774
- Who in God's name do you think you are?
- John Hammond.

106
00:10:23,014 --> 00:10:27,173
And I'm delighted to meet you finally
in person, Dr. Grant.

107
00:10:27,413 --> 00:10:32,493
So, I can see that my $50,000 a year
has been well spent.

108
00:10:32,732 --> 00:10:33,732
Who's the jerk?

109
00:10:33,972 --> 00:10:37,293
- This is our paleobotanist. Dr.--
- Sattler.

110
00:10:37,533 --> 00:10:39,813
Ellie, this is Mr. Hammond.

111
00:10:40,052 --> 00:10:42,693
I'm sorry about the dramatic entrance,
Dr. Sattler, but...

112
00:10:42,932 --> 00:10:44,692
- Did I say jerk?
- ... we're in a hurry.

113
00:10:44,933 --> 00:10:48,614
Will you have a drink?
We won't let it get warm. Sit down.

114
00:10:48,852 --> 00:10:51,812
- Let me have this.
- I'll just get a glass or two. I can do it.

115
00:10:52,053 --> 00:10:55,052
- There's samples all over here.
- I know my way around the kitchen.

116
00:10:55,293 --> 00:10:58,613
Now, I'll get right to the point.

117
00:10:58,773 --> 00:11:01,173
I like you. Both of you.

118
00:11:01,413 --> 00:11:06,014
I can tell instantly about people. It's a gift.

119
00:11:07,653 --> 00:11:10,933
I own an island off the coast of Costa Rica.

120
00:11:11,173 --> 00:11:14,293
I've leased it from the government
and during the last five years...

121
00:11:14,533 --> 00:11:16,773
I've been setting up a biological preserve.

122
00:11:17,014 --> 00:11:19,133
Really spectacular. Spared no expense.

123
00:11:19,373 --> 00:11:22,533
Make the one I've got down in Kenya
look like a petting zoo.

124
00:11:22,774 --> 00:11:26,854
And there's no doubt our attractions
will drive kids out of their minds.

125
00:11:27,092 --> 00:11:29,932
- And what are those?
- Small versions of adults, honey.

126
00:11:30,173 --> 00:11:32,052
And not just kids, everyone.

127
00:11:32,293 --> 00:11:38,092
We're opening next year, if the lawyers
don't kill me first. Do you like lawyers?

128
00:11:38,332 --> 00:11:41,134
- I don't really know any.
- Don't really know any.

129
00:11:41,374 --> 00:11:46,014
I do, and there's a particular one right now,
who represents my investors.

130
00:11:46,254 --> 00:11:48,813
Says that they insist on outside opinions.

131
00:11:49,053 --> 00:11:50,494
What kind of opinions?

132
00:11:50,734 --> 00:11:53,453
Well, your kind,
not to put too fine a point on it.

133
00:11:53,693 --> 00:11:57,693
Let's face it, in your particular fields,
you're the top minds.

134
00:11:57,932 --> 00:12:00,253
And if I could just persuade you...

135
00:12:00,494 --> 00:12:03,374
to sign off on the park,
give it your endorsement...

136
00:12:03,614 --> 00:12:09,332
maybe even pen a wee testimonial,
I could get back on schedule.

137
00:12:09,374 --> 00:12:14,333
- Why would they care what we think?
- What kind of park is this?

138
00:12:15,293 --> 00:12:18,413
It's right up your alley.

139
00:12:19,575 --> 00:12:22,653
Why don't you come down,
just the pair of you, for the weekend?

140
00:12:22,894 --> 00:12:26,134
I'd love to have an opinion
of a paleobotanist as well.

141
00:12:26,374 --> 00:12:30,254
I've got a jet standing by at Choteau.

142
00:12:30,653 --> 00:12:33,453
I'm sorry. This isn't possible.

143
00:12:33,693 --> 00:12:35,134
We just dug up a new skeleton—

144
00:12:35,374 --> 00:12:39,374
I'd compensate you
by fully funding your dig...

145
00:12:39,613 --> 00:12:42,092
- This is a very unusual time.
- The timing is—

146
00:12:42,334 --> 00:12:46,052
for a further three years.

147
00:12:49,373 --> 00:12:51,852
Where's the plane?

148
00:12:52,092 --> 00:12:53,052
- Okay.
- Three years.

149
00:12:53,293 --> 00:12:55,774
Cheers.

150
00:13:35,014 --> 00:13:37,533
Dodgson!

151
00:13:44,094 --> 00:13:46,534
You shouldn't use my name.

152
00:13:46,774 --> 00:13:50,334
Dodgson. We've got Dodgson here.

153
00:13:50,573 --> 00:13:53,533
See, nobody cares. Nice hat.

154
00:13:53,774 --> 00:13:56,533
What're you trying to look like,
a secret agent?

155
00:13:56,774 --> 00:13:58,533
- Well?
- $750,000.

156
00:13:58,774 --> 00:14:00,974
On delivery, $50,000 more
for each viable embryo.

157
00:14:01,214 --> 00:14:04,053
That's $1.5 million
if you get all 15 species off the island.

158
00:14:04,293 --> 00:14:05,134
I'll get them all.

159
00:14:05,374 --> 00:14:08,974
Remember, viable embryos.
They're no use to us if they don't survive.

160
00:14:09,214 --> 00:14:12,854
How am I supposed to transport them?

161
00:14:14,975 --> 00:14:17,735
The bottom screws open.

162
00:14:17,974 --> 00:14:19,654
That's great. Oh, my God.

163
00:14:19,894 --> 00:14:22,294
It's cooled and compartmentalised inside.

164
00:14:22,533 --> 00:14:25,653
- You guys. That's great.
- Customs can even check it if they want to.

165
00:14:25,894 --> 00:14:27,774
- Let me see.
- Go on.

166
00:14:28,014 --> 00:14:30,815
- There's enough coolant for 36 hours.
- No menthol?

167
00:14:31,053 --> 00:14:33,855
The embryos have to be back here
in San José by then.

168
00:14:34,094 --> 00:14:35,615
That's up to your guy on the boat.

169
00:14:35,854 --> 00:14:39,254
7:00 tomorrow night on the east dock.
Make sure he gets it right.

170
00:14:39,494 --> 00:14:43,213
- How are you planning to beat security?
- I've got an 18-minute window.

171
00:14:43,455 --> 00:14:48,413
18 minutes and your company
catches up on 10 years of research.

172
00:14:51,134 --> 00:14:54,534
Don't get cheap on me, Dodgson.

173
00:14:55,495 --> 00:14:58,735
That was Hammond's mistake.

174
00:15:10,933 --> 00:15:15,095
So you two dig up dinosaurs?

175
00:15:15,815 --> 00:15:19,134
- Well...
- We try to.

176
00:15:23,734 --> 00:15:26,094
You'll have to get used to Dr. Malcolm.

177
00:15:26,333 --> 00:15:30,374
He suffers from a deplorable excess
of personality, for a mathematician.

178
00:15:30,614 --> 00:15:32,335
Chaotician. Chaotician, actually.

179
00:15:32,575 --> 00:15:34,134
John doesn't subscribe to chaos...

180
00:15:34,375 --> 00:15:36,894
particularly what it says
about his science project.

181
00:15:37,134 --> 00:15:40,174
Ian, you've never been able to
sufficiently explain your concerns—

182
00:15:40,414 --> 00:15:43,014
Because of the behaviour
of the system in phase space?

183
00:15:43,254 --> 00:15:46,375
A load, if I may say so,
of fashionable number crunching—

184
00:15:46,615 --> 00:15:47,533
Don't.

185
00:15:47,774 --> 00:15:50,774
Dr. Sattler, Dr. Grant,
you've heard of Chaos Theory?

186
00:15:51,014 --> 00:15:51,774
No.

187
00:15:52,014 --> 00:15:54,413
No? Non-linear equations?

188
00:15:54,653 --> 00:15:57,573
Strange attractors?

189
00:15:58,014 --> 00:16:03,934
I refuse to believe that you aren't familiar
with the concept of attraction.

190
00:16:06,374 --> 00:16:11,014
I bring scientists. You bring a rock star.

191
00:16:13,815 --> 00:16:16,294
There it is.

192
00:16:41,573 --> 00:16:47,614
Bad wind shears. We have to drop
pretty fast. Hold on, this will be thrilling.

193
00:17:12,815 --> 00:17:15,214
But you need that piece over here,
and that piece...

194
00:17:15,453 --> 00:17:19,095
We'll have landed
by the time you get it right.

195
00:17:19,335 --> 00:17:21,974
Great.

196
00:18:35,295 --> 00:18:37,694
The full 50 miles of perimeter fence
are in place?

197
00:18:37,934 --> 00:18:40,855
And the concrete moats,
and the motion-sensor tracking systems.

198
00:18:41,095 --> 00:18:44,174
Donald, dear boy, relax.
Try and enjoy yourself.

199
00:18:44,414 --> 00:18:47,056
Let's get this straight.
This is not a weekend excursion.

200
00:18:47,296 --> 00:18:50,095
This is a serious investigation
of the stability of the island.

201
00:18:50,336 --> 00:18:53,175
Your investors, whom I represent,
are deeply concerned.

202
00:18:53,414 --> 00:18:56,375
48 hours from now,
if they're not convinced, I'm not convinced.

203
00:18:56,615 --> 00:18:58,615
I'll shut you down, John.

204
00:18:58,855 --> 00:19:04,336
In 48 hours,
I'll be accepting your apologies.

205
00:19:11,815 --> 00:19:15,494
All right, slow down. Slow. Stop.

206
00:19:16,135 --> 00:19:19,055
This shouldn't be here.

207
00:19:36,776 --> 00:19:39,335
Alan, this species of veriforman
has been extinct, since...

208
00:19:39,576 --> 00:19:42,056
the Cretaceous Period.
I mean, this thing...

209
00:19:42,296 --> 00:19:45,056
What?

210
00:20:12,016 --> 00:20:14,695
Look at that.

211
00:20:16,455 --> 00:20:20,056
It's a dinosaur.

212
00:20:22,575 --> 00:20:26,815
You did it. Crazy son of a bitch, you did it.

213
00:20:27,055 --> 00:20:30,336
We could tear up the rule book
on cold-bloodedness. It doesn't apply.

214
00:20:30,576 --> 00:20:32,694
They're wrong.
This is a warm-blooded creature.

215
00:20:32,935 --> 00:20:36,335
- This thing doesn't live in a swamp.
- This thing's got a 25, 27-foot neck?

216
00:20:36,575 --> 00:20:40,496
- The Brachiosaurus? 30.
- 30 feet.

217
00:20:54,935 --> 00:20:58,855
We're gonna make a fortune
with this place.

218
00:21:02,096 --> 00:21:03,375
How fast are they?

219
00:21:03,615 --> 00:21:08,175
Well, we clocked the T. Rex
at 32 miles an hour.

220
00:21:08,494 --> 00:21:10,655
T. Rex?

221
00:21:10,895 --> 00:21:13,615
You said you've got a T. Rex?

222
00:21:13,855 --> 00:21:15,695
Say again.

223
00:21:15,934 --> 00:21:18,694
We have a T. Rex.

224
00:21:19,095 --> 00:21:21,336
Put your head between your knees.

225
00:21:21,576 --> 00:21:22,976
Dr. Grant...

226
00:21:23,215 --> 00:21:25,175
my dear Dr. Sattler...

227
00:21:25,415 --> 00:21:30,576
welcome to Jurassic Park.

228
00:21:51,174 --> 00:21:54,375
They're moving in herds.

229
00:21:55,056 --> 00:21:58,055
They do move in herds.

230
00:22:04,297 --> 00:22:07,336
How did you do this?

231
00:22:08,537 --> 00:22:11,297
I'll show you.

232
00:22:33,215 --> 00:22:35,296
G'day.

233
00:22:35,536 --> 00:22:39,217
The most advanced amusement park
in the entire world...

234
00:22:39,455 --> 00:22:41,737
incorporating all the latest technologies.

235
00:22:41,977 --> 00:22:44,977
And I'm not talking just about rides.
Everybody has rides.

236
00:22:45,215 --> 00:22:49,175
No, we've made living
biological attractions so astounding...

237
00:22:49,416 --> 00:22:52,935
that they'll capture the imagination
of the entire planet.

238
00:22:53,175 --> 00:22:55,856
So, what are you thinking?

239
00:22:56,096 --> 00:23:00,375
- That we're out of a job.
- Don't you mean "extinct"?

240
00:23:06,655 --> 00:23:11,096
Why don't you all sit down?
Donald, sit down.

241
00:23:11,336 --> 00:23:15,775
Here he comes. Well, here I come.

242
00:23:18,777 --> 00:23:20,455
Hello.

243
00:23:20,696 --> 00:23:21,816
Say hello.

244
00:23:22,057 --> 00:23:24,057
Hello.

245
00:23:24,296 --> 00:23:25,856
Hello, John.

246
00:23:26,096 --> 00:23:27,656
Yes, I've got lines.

247
00:23:27,896 --> 00:23:32,415
Well, fine, I guess.
But, how did I get here?

248
00:23:32,655 --> 00:23:36,457
Well, let me show you.
First, I'll need a drop of blood.

249
00:23:36,695 --> 00:23:37,615
Your blood.

250
00:23:37,855 --> 00:23:40,297
Right.

251
00:23:40,576 --> 00:23:41,936
That hurt.

252
00:23:42,175 --> 00:23:45,096
Relax, John.
It's all part of the miracle of cloning.

253
00:23:45,336 --> 00:23:48,655
Hello, John.

254
00:23:49,096 --> 00:23:52,855
Cloned from what? Loy extraction
hasn't recreated an intact DNA strand.

255
00:23:53,096 --> 00:23:54,935
Not without massive sequence gaps.

256
00:23:55,176 --> 00:23:57,416
Paleo DNA, from what source?

257
00:23:57,656 --> 00:24:02,415
Where do you get 100 million-year-old
dinosaur blood?

258
00:24:05,576 --> 00:24:07,215
What? What?

259
00:24:07,457 --> 00:24:10,217
Well, Mr. DNA, where did you come from?

260
00:24:10,457 --> 00:24:11,537
From your blood.

261
00:24:11,776 --> 00:24:15,977
Just one drop of your blood
contains billions of strands of DNA...

262
00:24:16,217 --> 00:24:18,856
the building blocks of life.

263
00:24:19,096 --> 00:24:23,536
A DNA strand like me
is a blueprint for building a living thing.

264
00:24:23,777 --> 00:24:28,816
And sometimes animals that went extinct
millions of years ago, like dinosaurs...

265
00:24:29,056 --> 00:24:33,737
left their blueprints behind for us to find.
We just had to know where to look.

266
00:24:33,977 --> 00:24:38,497
100 million years ago,
there were mosquitoes just like today.

267
00:24:38,737 --> 00:24:41,936
And just like today,
they fed on the blood of animals.

268
00:24:42,176 --> 00:24:46,616
Even dinosaurs.
Sometimes, after biting a dinosaur...

269
00:24:46,855 --> 00:24:51,256
mosquitoes would land on the branch
of a tree and get stuck in the sap.

270
00:24:51,497 --> 00:24:56,096
After a long time, the tree sap
would get hard and become fossilised...

271
00:24:56,336 --> 00:25:01,457
just like a dinosaur bone,
preserving the mosquito inside.

272
00:25:01,695 --> 00:25:05,176
This fossilised tree sap,
which we call amber...

273
00:25:05,415 --> 00:25:09,457
waited for millions of years
with the mosquito inside...

274
00:25:09,695 --> 00:25:13,217
until Jurassic Park scientists came along.

275
00:25:13,457 --> 00:25:17,336
Using sophisticated techniques,
they extract the preserved blood...

276
00:25:17,576 --> 00:25:22,576
from the mosquito,
and bingo: dino DNA!

277
00:25:22,817 --> 00:25:27,257
A full DNA strand contains
three billion genetic codes.

278
00:25:27,497 --> 00:25:31,296
If we looked at screens like these
once a second for eight hours a day...

279
00:25:31,537 --> 00:25:35,376
it'd take two years
to look at the entire DNA strand.

280
00:25:35,616 --> 00:25:40,457
It's that long.
Since it's so old, it's full of holes.

281
00:25:40,697 --> 00:25:43,536
Now that's where our geneticists
take over.

282
00:25:43,776 --> 00:25:46,737
Thinking machine super-computers
and gene sequencers...

283
00:25:46,976 --> 00:25:49,256
break down the strand in minutes.

284
00:25:49,497 --> 00:25:51,298
And virtual-reality displays...

285
00:25:51,538 --> 00:25:54,896
show our geneticists the gaps
in the DNA sequence.

286
00:25:55,137 --> 00:25:57,376
We used the complete DNA of a frog...

287
00:25:57,617 --> 00:26:01,696
to fill in the holes and complete the code.

288
00:26:01,936 --> 00:26:07,416
Whew! And now,
we can make a baby dinosaur.

289
00:26:08,977 --> 00:26:13,176
This score is only temporary.
It all has very dramatic music, of course.

290
00:26:13,416 --> 00:26:15,736
A march or something.
It hasn't been written yet.

291
00:26:15,977 --> 00:26:18,936
And then, the tour moves on.

292
00:26:26,896 --> 00:26:31,337
Well, look here.
Those hard-working cowpokes...

293
00:26:31,577 --> 00:26:33,778
This is overwhelming, John.

294
00:26:34,017 --> 00:26:37,737
Are these characters autoerotica?

295
00:26:37,977 --> 00:26:40,538
No, we have no animatronics here.

296
00:26:40,777 --> 00:26:43,817
Those people are the real miracle workers
of Jurassic Park.

297
00:26:44,057 --> 00:26:47,656
in unfertilised emu or ostrich eggs.

298
00:26:47,896 --> 00:26:50,576
Wait a minute.
How do you interrupt the cellular mitosis?

299
00:26:50,817 --> 00:26:54,257
- Can't we see the unfertilised eggs?
- Shortly.

300
00:26:54,497 --> 00:26:58,096
Now a whole team of genetic engineers
goes to work on...

301
00:26:58,336 --> 00:27:01,416
- Can't you stop these things?
- I'm sorry. It's kind of a ride.

302
00:27:01,656 --> 00:27:05,017
One, two, three.

303
00:27:05,176 --> 00:27:06,496
You can't do that.

304
00:27:06,737 --> 00:27:10,176
- What?
- Can they do that?

305
00:27:11,136 --> 00:27:15,136
A reminder: the boat for the mainland
will be leaving at 19:00.

306
00:27:15,377 --> 00:27:20,616
All personnel be at the dock
no later than 18:45. No exceptions.

307
00:27:20,857 --> 00:27:25,416
- G'day, Henry.
- Good day, sir.

308
00:27:37,336 --> 00:27:40,336
It's turning the eggs.

309
00:27:42,297 --> 00:27:46,096
Perfect timing. I'd hoped they'd hatch
before I had to go to the boat.

310
00:27:46,336 --> 00:27:48,657
Henry, why didn't you tell me?

311
00:27:48,897 --> 00:27:53,136
I insist on being here when they're born.

312
00:27:53,377 --> 00:27:57,137
Come on.

313
00:27:57,137 --> 00:28:01,458
Come on, little one.

314
00:28:09,336 --> 00:28:12,577
Very good. Push.

315
00:28:12,978 --> 00:28:14,897
- Very good.
- God.

316
00:28:15,136 --> 00:28:17,778
Push. Come on.

317
00:28:18,017 --> 00:28:20,856
Come on. Come on, then.

318
00:28:21,096 --> 00:28:25,297
There you are. There.

319
00:28:28,657 --> 00:28:32,896
They imprint on the first creature
they come in contact with.

320
00:28:33,137 --> 00:28:34,857
Helps them to trust me.

321
00:28:35,096 --> 00:28:40,978
I've been present for the birth
of every creature on this island.

322
00:28:41,218 --> 00:28:44,218
Surely not the ones
that have bred in the wild.

323
00:28:44,457 --> 00:28:46,458
Actually they can't breed in the wild.

324
00:28:46,698 --> 00:28:49,257
Population control is one
of our security precautions.

325
00:28:49,497 --> 00:28:53,298
There's no unauthorised breeding
in Jurassic Park.

326
00:28:53,537 --> 00:28:56,537
How do you know they can't breed?

327
00:28:56,777 --> 00:28:59,856
Because all the animals
in Jurassic Park are female.

328
00:29:00,096 --> 00:29:02,297
We've engineered them that way.

329
00:29:02,537 --> 00:29:05,498
There you are.

330
00:29:06,218 --> 00:29:09,018
Oh, my God. Look at that.

331
00:29:09,258 --> 00:29:12,417
Blood temperature seems like
about high 80s, maybe.

332
00:29:12,657 --> 00:29:13,218
What?

333
00:29:13,458 --> 00:29:14,738
- 91.
- 91.

334
00:29:14,978 --> 00:29:16,978
Homeothermic? It holds that temperature?

335
00:29:17,218 --> 00:29:19,778
Yes.

336
00:29:22,696 --> 00:29:25,176
But, again, how do you know
they're all female?

337
00:29:25,417 --> 00:29:29,178
What, does somebody go out in the park
and pull up the dinosaurs' skirts?

338
00:29:29,416 --> 00:29:32,617
We control their chromosomes.
It's really not that difficult.

339
00:29:32,856 --> 00:29:35,018
All vertebrate embryos
are inherently female.

340
00:29:35,258 --> 00:29:38,777
They just require an extra hormone
given at the right developmental stage...

341
00:29:39,017 --> 00:29:39,938
to make them male.

342
00:29:40,176 --> 00:29:42,817
- We simply deny them that.
- Deny them that?

343
00:29:43,057 --> 00:29:46,337
John, the kind of control
you're attempting is not possible.

344
00:29:46,577 --> 00:29:49,298
If there's one thing the history
of evolution has taught us...

345
00:29:49,538 --> 00:29:53,617
it's that life will not be contained.
Life breaks free. Expands to new places...

346
00:29:53,857 --> 00:29:57,137
and it crashes through barriers,
painfully, maybe even dangerously...

347
00:29:57,377 --> 00:30:01,458
but there it is.

348
00:30:01,458 --> 00:30:02,897
There it is.

349
00:30:03,137 --> 00:30:07,978
You're implying that a group composed
entirely of female animals will breed.

350
00:30:08,217 --> 00:30:13,938
No, I'm simply saying that life finds a way.

351
00:30:19,336 --> 00:30:22,617
What species is this?

352
00:30:23,138 --> 00:30:26,179
It's a Velociraptor.

353
00:30:28,698 --> 00:30:31,617
You bred Raptors?

354
00:30:42,738 --> 00:30:47,617
Dr. Grant! As I was saying, we laid on
lunch for you before you go into the park.

355
00:30:47,856 --> 00:30:52,257
- Our gourmet chef, Alejandro—
- What are they doing?

356
00:30:56,817 --> 00:30:59,458
Feeding them.

357
00:31:02,817 --> 00:31:08,498
Alejandro's prepared a delightful menu
for us. Chilean sea bass.

358
00:31:08,738 --> 00:31:11,218
Shall we?

359
00:31:48,138 --> 00:31:50,177
They should all be destroyed.

360
00:31:50,417 --> 00:31:51,338
Robert Muldoon.

361
00:31:51,578 --> 00:31:53,138
My game warden from Kenya.

362
00:31:53,378 --> 00:31:56,097
Bit of an alarmist, but knows more
about Raptors than anyone.

363
00:31:56,337 --> 00:31:57,857
What's their growth rate?

364
00:31:58,097 --> 00:32:00,498
They're lethal at eight months,
and I do mean lethal.

365
00:32:00,738 --> 00:32:04,018
I've hunted most things that can hunt you,
but how these things move...

366
00:32:04,258 --> 00:32:06,019
- Fast for a biped?
- Cheetah speed.

367
00:32:06,258 --> 00:32:09,018
50, 60 miles per hour
if they ever got out in the open.

368
00:32:09,258 --> 00:32:10,817
And they're astonishing jumpers.

369
00:32:11,057 --> 00:32:15,298
Yes. That's why we're taking
extreme precautions.

370
00:32:15,538 --> 00:32:17,696
Do they show intelligence?

371
00:32:17,938 --> 00:32:21,858
They show extreme intelligence.
Even problem-solving intelligence.

372
00:32:22,097 --> 00:32:23,538
Especially the big one.

373
00:32:23,778 --> 00:32:25,817
We bred eight originally,
but when she came in...

374
00:32:26,058 --> 00:32:29,097
she took over the pride
and killed all but two of the others.

375
00:32:29,338 --> 00:32:35,857
That one, when she looks at you,
you can see she's working things out.

376
00:32:36,097 --> 00:32:38,098
That's why we have to feed them like this.

377
00:32:38,337 --> 00:32:41,098
She had them all attacking the fences
when the feeders came.

378
00:32:41,337 --> 00:32:42,857
The fences are electrified, right?

379
00:32:43,097 --> 00:32:46,018
That's right, but they never attack
the same place twice.

380
00:32:46,258 --> 00:32:50,897
They were testing the fences
for weaknesses systematically.

381
00:32:51,137 --> 00:32:54,057
They remember.

382
00:33:03,377 --> 00:33:06,258
Yes. Well, who's hungry?

383
00:33:06,499 --> 00:33:09,817
More adventurous guests, of course,
can opt for our jungle river cruise...

384
00:33:10,058 --> 00:33:12,018
or for a close-up look at our majestic...

385
00:33:12,259 --> 00:33:15,458
None of these attractions are ready yet,
but the park will open...

386
00:33:15,699 --> 00:33:19,698
with the basic tour you are about to take,
and then other rides will come on-line...

387
00:33:19,939 --> 00:33:23,259
six or twelve months after that.
Absolutely spectacular designs.

388
00:33:23,499 --> 00:33:26,499
- Spared no expense.
- And we can charge anything we want.

389
00:33:26,738 --> 00:33:29,178
$2,000 a day, $10,000 a day,
and people will pay it.

390
00:33:29,419 --> 00:33:32,419
- And there's the merchandise—
- Donald.

391
00:33:32,657 --> 00:33:35,618
This park was not built to cater
only for the super rich.

392
00:33:35,859 --> 00:33:38,578
Everyone in the world has the right
to enjoy these animals.

393
00:33:38,818 --> 00:33:39,739
Sure. They will.

394
00:33:39,979 --> 00:33:44,778
We'll have a coupon day or something.

395
00:33:47,058 --> 00:33:53,098
The lack of humility before nature
that's being displayed here staggers me.

396
00:33:53,338 --> 00:33:56,538
I think things are a little different
than you and I had feared.

397
00:33:56,778 --> 00:33:58,138
I know. They're a lot worse.

398
00:33:58,377 --> 00:34:01,939
Now, wait a second,
we haven't even seen the park yet—

399
00:34:02,179 --> 00:34:06,138
Donald, let him talk. There's no reason,
I want to hear every viewpoint.

400
00:34:06,377 --> 00:34:10,699
Don't you see the danger, John,
inherent in what you're doing here?

401
00:34:10,938 --> 00:34:13,818
Genetic power's the most awesome force
the planet's ever seen...

402
00:34:14,059 --> 00:34:16,659
but you wield it like a kid
that's found his dad's gun.

403
00:34:16,898 --> 00:34:20,179
- It's hardly appropriate to start hurling—
- If I may.

404
00:34:20,418 --> 00:34:23,778
I'll tell you the problem
with the scientific power you're using here.

405
00:34:24,019 --> 00:34:27,778
It didn't require any discipline to attain it.

406
00:34:28,018 --> 00:34:31,298
You read what others had done
and you took the next step.

407
00:34:31,539 --> 00:34:33,458
You didn't earn the knowledge
for yourselves...

408
00:34:33,699 --> 00:34:36,739
so you don't take
any responsibility for it.

409
00:34:36,978 --> 00:34:40,818
You stood on the shoulders of geniuses to
accomplish something as fast as you could.

410
00:34:41,057 --> 00:34:44,818
Before you even knew what you had,
you patented it, and packaged it...

411
00:34:45,059 --> 00:34:50,179
and slapped it on a plastic lunch box,
and now you're selling it.

412
00:34:50,418 --> 00:34:52,458
I don't think you're giving us
our due credit.

413
00:34:52,697 --> 00:34:55,820
Our scientists have done things
which nobody has ever done before.

414
00:34:56,059 --> 00:34:59,018
But your scientists were so preoccupied
with whether they could...

415
00:34:59,260 --> 00:35:01,259
they didn't stop to think if they should.

416
00:35:01,498 --> 00:35:05,059
Condors.
Condors are on the verge of extinction.

417
00:35:05,300 --> 00:35:10,458
If I created condors on this island,
you wouldn't have anything to say.

418
00:35:10,699 --> 00:35:13,139
This isn't some species
that was obliterated...

419
00:35:13,378 --> 00:35:16,458
by deforestation or the building of a dam.

420
00:35:16,699 --> 00:35:22,938
Dinosaurs had their shot
and nature selected them for extinction.

421
00:35:23,139 --> 00:35:27,059
I don't understand this Luddite attitude.
Especially from a scientist.

422
00:35:27,298 --> 00:35:31,860
How can we stand in the light of discovery
and not act?

423
00:35:32,099 --> 00:35:35,460
What's so great about discovery?
It's a violent, penetrative act...

424
00:35:35,699 --> 00:35:39,938
that scars what it explores.
What you call discovery...

425
00:35:40,179 --> 00:35:43,378
I call the rape of the natural world.

426
00:35:43,619 --> 00:35:47,298
The question is, how can you know
anything about an extinct eco-system?

427
00:35:47,539 --> 00:35:50,259
How could you ever assume
that you can control it?

428
00:35:50,498 --> 00:35:54,579
You have poisonous plants in this building.
You picked them because they look good.

429
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:58,699
But these are aggressive living things
that have no idea what century they're in...

430
00:35:58,940 --> 00:36:03,298
and they'll defend themselves.
Violently, if necessary.

431
00:36:04,139 --> 00:36:10,659
Dr. Grant. If there's one person who
could appreciate what I am trying to do...

432
00:36:10,699 --> 00:36:15,699
The world has just changed so radically
and we're all running to catch up.

433
00:36:15,940 --> 00:36:20,179
I don't want to jump to any conclusions,
but dinosaurs and man...

434
00:36:20,420 --> 00:36:24,260
two species separated
by 65 million years of evolution...

435
00:36:24,498 --> 00:36:27,659
have just been suddenly
thrown back into the mix together.

436
00:36:27,898 --> 00:36:32,940
How can we possibly have
the slightest idea what to expect?

437
00:36:33,179 --> 00:36:35,139
I don't believe it.

438
00:36:35,378 --> 00:36:38,378
You're meant to come here
and defend me against these characters...

439
00:36:38,619 --> 00:36:42,498
and the only one I've got on my side
is the blood-sucking lawyer.

440
00:36:42,739 --> 00:36:45,338
Thank you.

441
00:36:50,619 --> 00:36:52,380
They're here.

442
00:36:52,619 --> 00:36:55,179
You four are gonna have a spot
of company out in the park.

443
00:36:55,420 --> 00:36:57,938
Spend a little time
with our target audience.

444
00:36:58,179 --> 00:36:59,298
Grandpa!

445
00:36:59,539 --> 00:37:02,139
Kids!

446
00:37:03,500 --> 00:37:07,219
- Wait! Careful with me.
- We missed you.

447
00:37:07,460 --> 00:37:10,858
- We loved the presents. They were great.
- Did you enjoy the helicopter?

448
00:37:11,099 --> 00:37:15,139
Yeah, it went down
and we all went right up.

449
00:37:16,820 --> 00:37:21,059
Now, kids, come away.
Not too close to the cars.

450
00:37:21,298 --> 00:37:24,059
Aren't they lovely?
Aren't they glorious, though.

451
00:37:24,298 --> 00:37:26,539
These will be your transports
for the afternoon.

452
00:37:26,778 --> 00:37:29,219
- No drivers?
- No, drivers. They're electric.

453
00:37:29,460 --> 00:37:32,219
They run on this track
in the middle of the roadway here.

454
00:37:32,460 --> 00:37:35,300
Totally non-polluting. Top of the line.
Spared no expense.

455
00:37:35,539 --> 00:37:37,860
It's an interactive CD-ROM!

456
00:37:38,099 --> 00:37:40,820
You touch the screen
and it talks about whatever you want.

457
00:37:41,059 --> 00:37:43,860
Lex, you're all right in there.
Dr. Sattler, come with me.

458
00:37:44,099 --> 00:37:45,780
Dr. Grant, come in the second car.

459
00:37:46,019 --> 00:37:49,980
I'm gonna ride with, Dr. Sattler.

460
00:37:59,579 --> 00:38:02,659
I read your book.

461
00:38:02,659 --> 00:38:04,539
That's great.

462
00:38:04,778 --> 00:38:09,460
Do you really think dinosaurs turned
into birds and that's where they went?

463
00:38:09,699 --> 00:38:13,940
A few species may have evolved
along those lines.

464
00:38:17,260 --> 00:38:20,579
They sure don't look like birds to me.

465
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:23,460
I heard that there was this meteor...

466
00:38:23,699 --> 00:38:27,579
hit the earth someplace down in Mexico
and made this big crater.

467
00:38:27,820 --> 00:38:30,579
Listen...

468
00:38:31,420 --> 00:38:35,099
Tim, which car were you planning on?

469
00:38:35,338 --> 00:38:38,539
Whichever one you are.

470
00:38:39,619 --> 00:38:44,498
I heard about this thing in Omni
about this meteor making all this heat.

471
00:38:44,739 --> 00:38:46,980
It made diamond dust
and that changed the weather.

472
00:38:47,221 --> 00:38:48,619
They died because of the weather.

473
00:38:48,860 --> 00:38:54,619
My teacher tells me about this book
by a guy named Bakker. He says—

474
00:38:55,019 --> 00:38:58,500
She said I should ride with you
'cause it'd be good for you.

475
00:38:58,739 --> 00:39:00,219
The boat is now loading.

476
00:39:00,460 --> 00:39:06,059
Everyone must be on the dock
for the 19:00 departure.

477
00:39:06,300 --> 00:39:08,980
National Weather Service
is tracking a tropical storm...

478
00:39:09,219 --> 00:39:11,938
about 75 miles west of us.

479
00:39:12,179 --> 00:39:13,739
Why didn't I build in Orlando?

480
00:39:13,980 --> 00:39:19,059
I'll keep an eye on it.
Maybe it'll swing south like the last one.

481
00:39:21,780 --> 00:39:25,099
Start the tour program.

482
00:39:32,780 --> 00:39:36,061
Hold onto your butts.

483
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:47,820
And why did they put the fibreglass thing?

484
00:39:56,780 --> 00:39:58,858
You want to join the future, right?

485
00:39:59,099 --> 00:40:01,139
God help us when the hands of engineers—

486
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:03,981
During your tour,
the appropriate information will be...

487
00:40:04,219 --> 00:40:06,621
automatically selected
and displayed for you.

488
00:40:06,860 --> 00:40:10,221
Simply touch the area of the screen
displaying the appropriate icon.

489
00:40:10,460 --> 00:40:11,541
Are we gonna hit that?

490
00:40:11,780 --> 00:40:15,260
Welcome to Jurassic Park.

491
00:40:17,740 --> 00:40:21,539
What have they got in there, King Kong?

492
00:40:33,059 --> 00:40:35,780
The voice you're now hearing
is Richard Kiley.

493
00:40:36,019 --> 00:40:37,059
We spared no expense.

494
00:40:37,300 --> 00:40:38,539
If you look to the right...

495
00:40:38,780 --> 00:40:44,061
you'll see a herd of the first dinosaurs
on our tour, called Dilophosaurus.

496
00:40:44,300 --> 00:40:46,380
- Dilophosaurus!
- Shit!

497
00:40:46,621 --> 00:40:48,181
One of the earliest carnivores...

498
00:40:48,420 --> 00:40:51,221
we now know Dilophosaurus
is actually poisonous...

499
00:40:51,460 --> 00:40:56,340
spitting its venom at its prey,
causing blindness and paralysis...

500
00:40:56,581 --> 00:40:59,940
allowing the carnivore
to eat at its leisure.

501
00:41:00,179 --> 00:41:03,659
This makes Dilophosaurus a beautiful
but deadly addition...

502
00:41:03,900 --> 00:41:07,141
to Jurassic Park.

503
00:41:12,500 --> 00:41:15,420
Alan?

504
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:22,181
Damn.

505
00:41:25,501 --> 00:41:27,501
Vehicle headlights are on
and not responding.

506
00:41:27,739 --> 00:41:29,981
Those shouldn't be running off
of car batteries.

507
00:41:30,221 --> 00:41:32,740
Item 151 on today's glitch list.

508
00:41:32,981 --> 00:41:35,621
We have all the problems
of a major theme park and a zoo...

509
00:41:35,860 --> 00:41:40,219
and the computers
aren't even on their feet yet.

510
00:41:40,581 --> 00:41:45,699
Our lives are in your hands
and you have butter fingers?

511
00:41:45,820 --> 00:41:48,059
I am totally unappreciated in my time.

512
00:41:48,300 --> 00:41:51,820
You can run this park from this room
with minimal staff for up to three days.

513
00:41:52,059 --> 00:41:54,780
You think that kind of automation is easy?

514
00:41:55,019 --> 00:41:55,659
Or cheap?

515
00:41:55,900 --> 00:41:58,300
You know anyone
who can network eight machines...

516
00:41:58,541 --> 00:42:02,221
and de-bug two million lines of code
on my salary? if so, I want to see them.

517
00:42:02,460 --> 00:42:06,541
Sorry about your financial problems,
I really am. But they are your problems.

518
00:42:06,780 --> 00:42:08,981
You're absolutely right.
Everything's my problem.

519
00:42:09,221 --> 00:42:14,460
I will not be drawn into another
financial debate with you. I really will not.

520
00:42:14,701 --> 00:42:16,541
There's been hardly any debate at all.

521
00:42:16,780 --> 00:42:20,061
I don't blame people for their mistakes.

522
00:42:20,300 --> 00:42:22,420
But I do ask that they pay for them.

523
00:42:22,661 --> 00:42:24,780
Thanks, Dad.

524
00:42:25,021 --> 00:42:25,820
The headlights.

525
00:42:26,059 --> 00:42:28,460
I'll de-bug the tour program
when they get back, okay?

526
00:42:28,701 --> 00:42:31,659
It'll eat a lot of compute cycles.
We'll lose part of the system.

527
00:42:31,900 --> 00:42:34,860
There's a small amount of memory,
you can't use it for everything.

528
00:42:35,099 --> 00:42:39,581
- Are you gonna compile for a half an hour—
- Quiet, all of you!

529
00:42:39,661 --> 00:42:43,260
They're approaching
the Tyrannosaur paddock.

530
00:43:30,701 --> 00:43:34,300
God creates dinosaurs.
God destroys dinosaurs.

531
00:43:34,541 --> 00:43:39,981
God creates man. Man destroys God.
Man creates dinosaurs.

532
00:43:40,219 --> 00:43:44,860
Dinosaurs eat man.

533
00:43:45,101 --> 00:43:48,581
Woman inherits the earth.

534
00:43:54,380 --> 00:43:58,940
We'll try to tempt the Rex now.
Keep watching the fence.

535
00:44:07,181 --> 00:44:10,460
What's going to happen to the goat?

536
00:44:13,141 --> 00:44:14,860
He's going to eat the goat?

537
00:44:15,101 --> 00:44:17,221
Excellent.

538
00:44:17,460 --> 00:44:20,940
What's the matter, kid?
You never had lamb chops?

539
00:44:21,181 --> 00:44:24,820
I happen to be a vegetarian.

540
00:44:31,340 --> 00:44:35,061
T. Rex doesn't want to be fed.
He wants to hunt.

541
00:44:35,302 --> 00:44:41,382
Can't just suppress 65 million years
of gut instinct.

542
00:45:01,141 --> 00:45:07,380
Eventually you do plan to have dinosaurs
on your dinosaur tour, right?

543
00:45:09,141 --> 00:45:11,701
Hello. Yes?

544
00:45:11,942 --> 00:45:15,342
I really hate that man.

545
00:45:18,860 --> 00:45:23,342
The Tyrannosaur doesn't obey
any set patterns or park schedules.

546
00:45:23,581 --> 00:45:26,541
The essence of chaos.

547
00:45:26,782 --> 00:45:30,141
I'm still not clear on chaos.

548
00:45:30,260 --> 00:45:35,501
It simply deals with unpredictability
in complex systems.

549
00:45:35,501 --> 00:45:37,260
The shorthand is the Butterfly Effect.

550
00:45:37,501 --> 00:45:39,460
A butterfly can flap its wings in Peking...

551
00:45:39,701 --> 00:45:43,900
and in Central Park
you get rain instead of sunshine.

552
00:45:44,780 --> 00:45:46,541
- Was I going too fast?
- I missed it.

553
00:45:46,780 --> 00:45:47,581
I did a fly-by.

554
00:45:47,820 --> 00:45:50,300
Give me that glass of water.
We'll conduct an experiment.

555
00:45:50,541 --> 00:45:53,822
The car should be still. But that's okay.
It's just an example.

556
00:45:54,061 --> 00:45:56,942
Put your hand flat like a hieroglyphic.

557
00:45:57,181 --> 00:45:58,742
A drop of water falls on your hand.

558
00:45:58,981 --> 00:46:01,661
Which way will the drop roll off?
Which finger?

559
00:46:01,902 --> 00:46:04,782
Thumb, I'd say.

560
00:46:05,900 --> 00:46:07,581
Freeze your hand. Don't move.

561
00:46:07,820 --> 00:46:10,581
I'll do the same thing,
start with the same place again.

562
00:46:10,822 --> 00:46:12,061
Which way, now?

563
00:46:12,302 --> 00:46:16,621
Let's say back the same way.

564
00:46:17,420 --> 00:46:19,621
It changed. Why?

565
00:46:19,862 --> 00:46:22,342
Because tiny variations...

566
00:46:22,581 --> 00:46:24,740
the orientation of the hairs
on your hands...

567
00:46:24,981 --> 00:46:26,101
Alan, look at this.

568
00:46:26,340 --> 00:46:29,902
the amount of blood distending
your vessels, imperfections in the skin...

569
00:46:30,141 --> 00:46:32,302
- Imperfections in the skin?
- Just microscopic.

570
00:46:32,541 --> 00:46:35,822
never repeat and vastly effect
the outcome. That's...

571
00:46:36,063 --> 00:46:39,501
- ... what?
- Unpredictability.

572
00:46:40,661 --> 00:46:43,541
There. Look at this. See? I'm right again.

573
00:46:43,782 --> 00:46:49,501
Nobody could predict that Dr. Grant
would jump out of a moving vehicle.

574
00:46:50,141 --> 00:46:53,621
And there's another example.

575
00:46:53,862 --> 00:46:59,222
See, here I am now by myself,
talking to myself. That's Chaos Theory.

576
00:46:59,461 --> 00:47:02,501
Stop the program.

577
00:47:04,181 --> 00:47:07,742
I told you how many times we needed
locking mechanisms on the vehicle doors.

578
00:47:07,983 --> 00:47:10,822
Stopping park vehicles
and rebooting program.

579
00:47:11,061 --> 00:47:14,742
We're on. Will notify for resume.

580
00:47:15,581 --> 00:47:17,422
I'll switch it over to TelePrompTer.

581
00:47:17,661 --> 00:47:22,101
- What about the circuit integrities?
- Screen says faulty sensor.

582
00:47:23,382 --> 00:47:26,782
- I'll call security.
- We're at full go on that and it looks good.

583
00:47:27,021 --> 00:47:29,862
Like I said, there's this other book
by a guy named Bakker...

584
00:47:30,101 --> 00:47:32,141
and he says dinosaurs died
from diseases.

585
00:47:32,382 --> 00:47:35,422
- Where are we going?
- He didn't say they turned into birds.

586
00:47:35,661 --> 00:47:37,541
Anyone else think
we shouldn't be out here?

587
00:47:37,782 --> 00:47:40,300
And his book was a lot fatter than yours.
Like this.

588
00:47:40,541 --> 00:47:44,380
- Really?
- Yours was fully illustrated.

589
00:47:44,621 --> 00:47:45,780
You okay?

590
00:47:46,021 --> 00:47:48,782
Look at this. Wait. Watch.

591
00:47:49,023 --> 00:47:52,262
Come on. Watch this.

592
00:47:56,262 --> 00:47:59,222
Everybody stay here.

593
00:48:15,222 --> 00:48:18,820
- Okay?
- Of course.

594
00:48:20,141 --> 00:48:23,661
Don't be scared. Come on, it's okay.
Muldoon tranquillised her for me.

595
00:48:23,900 --> 00:48:26,621
She's sick.

596
00:48:33,661 --> 00:48:36,501
Oh, my God.

597
00:48:56,702 --> 00:48:58,902
She was always my favourite
when I was a kid...

598
00:48:59,141 --> 00:49:03,862
and now I see
she's the most beautiful thing I ever saw.

599
00:49:06,541 --> 00:49:09,342
It's okay.

600
00:49:10,862 --> 00:49:14,822
Microvesicles. That's interesting.

601
00:49:15,262 --> 00:49:17,782
Thanks. What are her symptoms?

602
00:49:18,023 --> 00:49:20,581
Imbalance, disorientation,
laboured breathing.

603
00:49:20,822 --> 00:49:22,742
It seems to happen every six weeks or so.

604
00:49:22,983 --> 00:49:25,782
Six weeks.

605
00:49:37,382 --> 00:49:38,782
- These are dilated.
- They are?

606
00:49:39,021 --> 00:49:42,141
- Take a look.
- I'll be damned.

607
00:49:42,382 --> 00:49:47,463
That's pharmacological.
From local plant life.

608
00:49:53,103 --> 00:49:55,342
Is this West lndian lilac?

609
00:49:55,583 --> 00:50:00,461
Yes. We know they're toxic,
but the animals don't eat them.

610
00:50:03,222 --> 00:50:07,143
- Are you sure?
- Pretty sure.

611
00:50:07,382 --> 00:50:09,942
There's only one way to be positive.

612
00:50:10,181 --> 00:50:13,501
I'd have to see the dinosaur's droppings.

613
00:50:13,742 --> 00:50:18,222
Dino droppings? Droppings?

614
00:50:22,302 --> 00:50:25,222
Yeah, I got that.

615
00:50:25,463 --> 00:50:28,503
That storm centre hasn't dissipated
or changed course.

616
00:50:28,742 --> 00:50:30,943
We're gonna have to cut the tour short.

617
00:50:31,182 --> 00:50:32,742
We'll pick it up again tomorrow.

618
00:50:32,983 --> 00:50:35,943
- Are you sure we have to?
- It's not worth taking the chance.

619
00:50:36,182 --> 00:50:39,503
- Sustained winds at 45 knots.
- Tell them when they get back in the cars.

620
00:50:39,742 --> 00:50:44,061
Ladies and gentlemen last shuttle leaving
for the dock leaves in five minutes.

621
00:50:44,302 --> 00:50:48,023
Drop what you're doing and leave now.

622
00:50:48,943 --> 00:50:51,543
Damn.

623
00:50:57,182 --> 00:51:00,503
That is one big pile of shit.

624
00:51:06,063 --> 00:51:08,422
You're right.
There's no trace of lilac berries.

625
00:51:08,663 --> 00:51:10,822
That's so odd, though.

626
00:51:11,063 --> 00:51:15,702
All right,
she's suffering from Melia toxicity...

627
00:51:16,182 --> 00:51:19,862
every six weeks.

628
00:51:19,943 --> 00:51:24,023
Let's see, rats.

629
00:51:24,262 --> 00:51:27,943
She's tenacious.

630
00:51:28,182 --> 00:51:31,302
You have no idea.

631
00:51:31,822 --> 00:51:37,061
You will remember to wash your hands
before you eat anything?

632
00:51:47,302 --> 00:51:52,543
Doctors, if you please,
I have to insist that we get moving.

633
00:51:53,382 --> 00:51:57,302
I'd like to stay with Dr. Harding
and finish up with the Trike.

634
00:51:57,543 --> 00:51:59,103
Sure. I'm in a gas-powered jeep.

635
00:51:59,342 --> 00:52:01,822
I'll drop her off at the centre
before I make the boat.

636
00:52:02,063 --> 00:52:04,623
Great. Then I'll catch up with you later.

637
00:52:04,862 --> 00:52:05,623
You sure?

638
00:52:05,862 --> 00:52:08,382
Yeah, I want to stay
with her a little longer.

639
00:52:08,623 --> 00:52:11,461
Okay, then.

640
00:52:24,182 --> 00:52:28,143
There's nothing I can do.
The captain says we have to go, we go.

641
00:52:28,382 --> 00:52:29,862
You've got to give me time.

642
00:52:30,103 --> 00:52:33,463
I did a test run, it took me 20 minutes.
I think I can push it to 18 but...

643
00:52:33,702 --> 00:52:35,583
you've got to give me
at least 15 minutes.

644
00:52:35,822 --> 00:52:38,583
No promises.

645
00:52:44,543 --> 00:52:48,503
Visitor vehicles are returning
to the garage.

646
00:52:49,262 --> 00:52:55,181
So much for our first tour.
Two no-shows and one sick Triceratops.

647
00:52:55,422 --> 00:52:57,983
It could've been worse.

648
00:52:58,222 --> 00:52:59,342
A lot worse.

649
00:52:59,583 --> 00:53:03,344
Anybody want a soda or something?
I'm going up to the machine.

650
00:53:03,583 --> 00:53:05,503
I thought maybe
I'd get somebody something.

651
00:53:05,742 --> 00:53:09,902
I've had only sweets
and I'm gonna get something salty.

652
00:53:10,143 --> 00:53:13,302
I finished de-bugging the phones.

653
00:53:13,543 --> 00:53:15,503
I was going to, so I did.

654
00:53:15,744 --> 00:53:18,503
So I de-bugged the phones.
And I thought maybe...

655
00:53:18,742 --> 00:53:21,222
I should tell you the system
will be compiling...

656
00:53:21,463 --> 00:53:24,063
for 18 to 20 minutes,
so some of the minor systems...

657
00:53:24,302 --> 00:53:29,262
they might go on and off for a while,
but it's nothing to worry about.

658
00:53:49,184 --> 00:53:54,742
- Do you have any kids?
- Me? Hell, yeah. Three. I love kids.

659
00:53:55,143 --> 00:53:57,663
Anything at all can and does happen.

660
00:53:57,902 --> 00:54:00,463
Same with wives, for that matter.

661
00:54:00,702 --> 00:54:04,543
- You're married?
- Occasionally.

662
00:54:05,023 --> 00:54:10,702
I'm always on the lookout
for a future ex-Mrs. Malcolm.

663
00:54:22,224 --> 00:54:26,023
Five, four...

664
00:54:36,224 --> 00:54:38,423
- That's odd.
- What?

665
00:54:38,663 --> 00:54:40,822
Door security systems are shutting down.

666
00:54:41,063 --> 00:54:45,623
Nedry said a few systems
would go off-line, didn't he?

667
00:55:37,985 --> 00:55:40,623
By the way, Dr. Sattler...

668
00:55:40,864 --> 00:55:45,623
- ... she's not, like, available, is she?
- Why?

669
00:55:46,943 --> 00:55:51,103
- I'm sorry. You two are...
- Yeah.

670
00:55:52,864 --> 00:55:57,943
- What'd I touch?
- You didn't touch anything. We stopped.

671
00:56:04,983 --> 00:56:06,463
What the hell?

672
00:56:06,702 --> 00:56:09,744
- What now?
- Fences are failing all over the park.

673
00:56:09,983 --> 00:56:13,543
Find Nedry. Check the vending machines.

674
00:56:42,664 --> 00:56:44,503
Just look at this work station.

675
00:56:44,744 --> 00:56:49,585
- What a complete slob.
- The Raptor fences aren't out, are they?

676
00:56:54,824 --> 00:56:57,983
No, no. They're still on.

677
00:56:59,025 --> 00:57:02,943
Why the hell would he turn
the other ones off?

678
00:57:39,905 --> 00:57:42,063
Access main program.

679
00:57:42,304 --> 00:57:44,384
Access main security.

680
00:57:44,623 --> 00:57:47,744
Access main program grid.

681
00:57:55,023 --> 00:57:57,184
You didn't say the magic word.

682
00:57:57,423 --> 00:58:00,704
Please! Goddamn it.
I hate this hacker crap.

683
00:58:00,943 --> 00:58:05,543
Phone Nedry's people in Cambridge.

684
00:58:06,385 --> 00:58:09,344
Phones are out, too.

685
00:58:11,264 --> 00:58:14,344
Where did the vehicles stop?

686
00:58:33,144 --> 00:58:34,864
Their radio's out, too.

687
00:58:35,103 --> 00:58:37,184
- Gennaro said to stay put.
- Kids okay?

688
00:58:37,423 --> 00:58:39,344
I didn't ask. Why wouldn't they be?

689
00:58:39,585 --> 00:58:41,063
- Kids get scared.
- What's scary?

690
00:58:41,304 --> 00:58:44,103
- It's just a little hiccup in the power.
- I'm not scared.

691
00:58:44,344 --> 00:58:47,784
- I didn't say you were scared.
- I know.

692
00:58:49,784 --> 00:58:51,985
Hey, where did you find those?

693
00:58:52,224 --> 00:58:54,905
- In the box, under my seat.
- Are they heavy?

694
00:58:55,144 --> 00:58:59,184
- Yeah.
- Then they're expensive. Put them back.

695
00:59:04,583 --> 00:59:07,545
Don't scare me.

696
00:59:17,103 --> 00:59:20,304
Cool. Night vision.

697
00:59:43,025 --> 00:59:45,864
Did you feel that?

698
01:00:16,985 --> 01:00:20,985
Maybe it's the power
trying to come back on.

699
01:00:22,025 --> 01:00:24,744
What is that?

700
01:00:39,704 --> 01:00:42,505
Where's the goat?

701
01:01:03,144 --> 01:01:05,784
Jesus!

702
01:01:08,985 --> 01:01:12,184
He left us.

703
01:01:21,545 --> 01:01:27,186
- Where does he think he's going?
- When you gotta go, you gotta go.

704
01:01:43,065 --> 01:01:45,784
Dr. Grant.

705
01:01:56,664 --> 01:02:00,186
I hate being right all the time.

706
01:02:07,786 --> 01:02:13,266
Keep absolutely still.
Its vision is based on movement.

707
01:02:25,226 --> 01:02:28,184
Turn the light off.

708
01:02:31,425 --> 01:02:34,304
Turn the light off!

709
01:03:26,385 --> 01:03:30,626
- Hurry! Turn it off!
- I'm sorry!

710
01:03:33,144 --> 01:03:36,545
You're the one who turned it—

711
01:04:50,826 --> 01:04:52,346
Ian, freeze!

712
01:04:52,585 --> 01:04:54,626
Get the kids!

713
01:04:54,865 --> 01:04:57,826
- Get rid of the flare!
- Get the kids!

714
01:04:58,065 --> 01:05:01,065
Get rid of the flare!

715
01:05:02,425 --> 01:05:05,666
Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord be—

716
01:05:25,706 --> 01:05:28,385
Dr. Grant!

717
01:05:31,346 --> 01:05:33,666
I'm stuck. The seat's got my feet.

718
01:05:33,905 --> 01:05:37,186
I'll get you next. You're okay.

719
01:05:37,425 --> 01:05:39,226
You're all right.

720
01:05:39,465 --> 01:05:42,465
Tim. Tim!

721
01:05:44,545 --> 01:05:48,826
Don't move.
He can't see us if we don't move.

722
01:06:50,067 --> 01:06:53,545
Lex! Lex, you're choking me.

723
01:06:54,786 --> 01:06:57,465
Grab the wire.

724
01:07:14,907 --> 01:07:18,505
Keycheck space minus 0.
Keychecks off, safety space minus 0.

725
01:07:18,746 --> 01:07:21,666
He's turning the safety systems off.
Doesn't want anybody to see.

726
01:07:21,907 --> 01:07:24,907
Look at this next entry.
It's the kicker. "White rabbit object."

727
01:07:25,146 --> 01:07:28,786
It did it all. With the keychecks off,
the computer didn't file the keystrokes.

728
01:07:29,027 --> 01:07:32,467
The way to find them is to check
the computer's lines of code one by one.

729
01:07:32,706 --> 01:07:35,746
- How many lines of code are there?
- About two million.

730
01:07:35,985 --> 01:07:38,826
- Two million?
- Yeah.

731
01:07:40,186 --> 01:07:46,188
Robert, I wonder if perhaps you would
be good enough to take a gas jeep...

732
01:07:46,226 --> 01:07:48,987
and bring back my grandchildren.

733
01:07:49,226 --> 01:07:51,706
Sure.

734
01:07:53,027 --> 01:07:55,947
I'm going with him.

735
01:07:57,666 --> 01:08:00,146
John.

736
01:08:04,387 --> 01:08:09,226
I can't get Jurassic Park back on-line
without Dennis Nedry.

737
01:08:27,067 --> 01:08:29,667
Damn it!

738
01:08:34,627 --> 01:08:37,466
There's the road!

739
01:08:42,266 --> 01:08:45,987
Winch this sucker off the thing
and then I tie it to the that thing there.

740
01:08:46,226 --> 01:08:50,987
I'll pull down this thing here,
and pull it back up again.

741
01:08:57,466 --> 01:09:02,266
My glasses. I can afford more glasses!

742
01:09:28,787 --> 01:09:33,466
You've got time. You can do it.
Do it. Come on, Dennis.

743
01:09:39,105 --> 01:09:41,586
Hello.

744
01:09:45,027 --> 01:09:49,188
Yeah, that's nice. I've got to go.

745
01:09:58,466 --> 01:09:59,908
Nice boy.

746
01:10:00,146 --> 01:10:01,506
Nice boy.

747
01:10:01,747 --> 01:10:03,867
Nice dinosaur.

748
01:10:04,106 --> 01:10:07,226
Thought you were one of your big brothers.
You're not so bad.

749
01:10:07,466 --> 01:10:11,067
What do you want? A little food?
Look at me. I just fell down a hill.

750
01:10:11,306 --> 01:10:15,747
I'm soaking wet. I don't have any food.
I have nothing on me.

751
01:10:15,985 --> 01:10:19,587
Go on. Play fetch?

752
01:10:20,186 --> 01:10:22,828
Look, stick.

753
01:10:22,948 --> 01:10:25,427
Look at stick.

754
01:10:25,667 --> 01:10:28,948
Stick, stupid.
Fetch the stick, boy. Look at the stick.

755
01:10:29,186 --> 01:10:31,346
You like the stick? Go on and get it.

756
01:10:31,587 --> 01:10:34,307
No wonder you're extinct.

757
01:10:34,546 --> 01:10:38,188
I'm gonna run you over
when I come back down.

758
01:11:50,028 --> 01:11:53,028
Timmy!

759
01:11:55,467 --> 01:11:57,868
Now, Lex. Listen.

760
01:11:58,108 --> 01:12:00,106
Lex, I'm right here.

761
01:12:00,347 --> 01:12:02,587
I'll look after you.
I have to help your brother.

762
01:12:02,827 --> 01:12:05,067
Stay right here and wait for me.

763
01:12:05,306 --> 01:12:07,787
He left us.

764
01:12:08,028 --> 01:12:11,907
But that's not what I'm going to do.

765
01:12:12,386 --> 01:12:14,868
Okay?

766
01:12:15,867 --> 01:12:18,427
Stay here.

767
01:12:43,068 --> 01:12:45,346
You hear me? I'm coming up.

768
01:12:45,587 --> 01:12:50,867
I hate climbing. I hate trees, way too high.

769
01:13:16,507 --> 01:13:19,028
You okay?

770
01:13:20,427 --> 01:13:23,108
I threw up.

771
01:13:24,627 --> 01:13:26,467
That's okay.

772
01:13:26,707 --> 01:13:29,827
Just give me your hand.

773
01:13:32,347 --> 01:13:37,708
Tim, I won't tell anyone you threw up.
Just give me your hand.

774
01:13:44,908 --> 01:13:47,667
Here, I've got you.

775
01:13:47,908 --> 01:13:49,188
Okay.

776
01:13:49,427 --> 01:13:54,587
- That's good. Over the rail.
- Don't pull me too hard.

777
01:13:57,467 --> 01:14:01,108
Stand on the door. Hang on to me.

778
01:14:03,108 --> 01:14:05,547
- That's not too bad, right, Timmy?
- Yes, it is.

779
01:14:05,787 --> 01:14:08,228
It's like a tree house.
Your dad ever build you one?

780
01:14:08,466 --> 01:14:11,427
- No.
- Yeah, me, too.

781
01:14:11,547 --> 01:14:14,069
The thing about climbing is,
you never look down.

782
01:14:14,309 --> 01:14:15,868
This is impossible.

783
01:14:16,109 --> 01:14:21,667
- How am I going to do this? It's about—
- I'm going to help you with your footing.

784
01:14:25,708 --> 01:14:28,189
Tim, go.

785
01:14:48,386 --> 01:14:51,266
Go, Tim, go!

786
01:14:57,747 --> 01:15:00,389
Faster, Tim!

787
01:15:05,349 --> 01:15:07,908
Tim, jump!

788
01:15:10,268 --> 01:15:12,788
Go, Tim.

789
01:15:19,427 --> 01:15:23,228
We're back in the car again.

790
01:15:23,868 --> 01:15:27,347
At least you're out of the tree.

791
01:15:31,029 --> 01:15:34,068
Where's the other car?

792
01:15:35,707 --> 01:15:37,668
Alan!

793
01:15:37,908 --> 01:15:40,588
Dr. Grant!

794
01:15:54,588 --> 01:15:59,347
- I think this was Gennaro.
- I think this was, too.

795
01:16:01,307 --> 01:16:03,587
- I think it's ahead of us.
- It could be anywhere.

796
01:16:03,828 --> 01:16:08,547
With fences down,
it can wander in and out of any paddock.

797
01:16:13,788 --> 01:16:17,189
He's put a tourniquet on. Ian.

798
01:16:17,349 --> 01:16:21,429
Remind me to thank John
for a lovely weekend.

799
01:16:24,748 --> 01:16:27,469
Can we chance moving him?

800
01:16:27,707 --> 01:16:30,587
Please chance it.

801
01:16:37,307 --> 01:16:40,307
Ellie, come on!

802
01:16:46,068 --> 01:16:47,587
The other car!

803
01:16:47,829 --> 01:16:50,509
Dr. Grant?

804
01:16:53,349 --> 01:16:56,269
They're not here.

805
01:17:22,948 --> 01:17:25,868
Anybody hear that?

806
01:17:27,828 --> 01:17:30,750
It's an impact tremor, is what it is.

807
01:17:30,988 --> 01:17:33,868
I'm fairly alarmed here.

808
01:17:34,109 --> 01:17:38,149
Come on. We've got to get out of here.
Now!

809
01:17:38,429 --> 01:17:40,948
Let's go.

810
01:17:50,469 --> 01:17:53,189
Must go faster.

811
01:17:54,269 --> 01:17:58,469
Here it comes! Stand on it! Fifth gear!

812
01:18:03,068 --> 01:18:07,548
- Get off the stick! Bloody move!
- Look out!

813
01:18:32,069 --> 01:18:35,788
Think they'll have that on the tour?

814
01:18:42,149 --> 01:18:45,149
Are you hearing this?

815
01:18:49,989 --> 01:18:52,750
Come on, Tim. Hurry up.

816
01:18:52,988 --> 01:18:56,109
Let's get up this tree.

817
01:18:57,469 --> 01:19:01,588
Come on, Tim, it's okay.
Listen. Try up here.

818
01:19:04,269 --> 01:19:05,949
I hate trees.

819
01:19:06,189 --> 01:19:11,149
- They don't bother me.
- Yeah? Well, you weren't in the last one.

820
01:19:23,590 --> 01:19:28,469
Look at the Brontosauruses.
I mean, Brachiosaurus.

821
01:19:28,469 --> 01:19:31,310
They're singing.

822
01:19:57,868 --> 01:19:59,469
Don't let the monsters come here.

823
01:19:59,708 --> 01:20:02,989
They're not monsters, they're animals.
These are herbivores.

824
01:20:03,229 --> 01:20:06,310
They only eat vegetables.
For you, I think they'd make an exception.

825
01:20:06,550 --> 01:20:09,628
I hate the other kind.

826
01:20:10,149 --> 01:20:14,309
The other kind just do what they do.

827
01:20:32,109 --> 01:20:34,748
What's that?

828
01:20:38,270 --> 01:20:43,390
What are you and Ellie going to do,
if you don't dig up bones anymore?

829
01:20:43,430 --> 01:20:45,590
I don't know.

830
01:20:45,829 --> 01:20:49,630
I guess we'll just have to evolve, too.

831
01:20:51,670 --> 01:20:55,270
What do you call a blind dinosaur?

832
01:20:55,708 --> 01:21:00,470
- What do you call a blind dinosaur?
- Do-you-think-he-saurus.

833
01:21:03,109 --> 01:21:06,069
What do you call a blind dinosaur's dog?

834
01:21:06,309 --> 01:21:07,509
You got me.

835
01:21:07,750 --> 01:21:11,149
A "Do-you-think-he-saurus' Rex."

836
01:21:12,270 --> 01:21:14,750
- Alan?
- Yeah?

837
01:21:14,988 --> 01:21:19,510
What if the dinosaurs come back
while we're all asleep?

838
01:21:20,230 --> 01:21:23,829
- I'll stay awake.
- All night?

839
01:21:24,829 --> 01:21:27,390
All night.

840
01:22:14,109 --> 01:22:17,149
They were all melting.

841
01:22:19,229 --> 01:22:23,710
Malcolm's okay for now.
I gave him a shot of morphine.

842
01:22:25,949 --> 01:22:28,149
They'll all be fine.

843
01:22:28,390 --> 01:22:33,989
Who better to get the children through
Jurassic Park than a dinosaur expert?

844
01:22:36,791 --> 01:22:39,470
You know, the first...

845
01:22:39,710 --> 01:22:44,510
attraction I ever built
when I came down from Scotland...

846
01:22:44,630 --> 01:22:48,109
was a flea circus, Petticoat Lane.

847
01:22:48,350 --> 01:22:50,390
Really quite wonderful.

848
01:22:50,631 --> 01:22:55,229
I had a wee trapeze and a...

849
01:22:56,829 --> 01:22:59,470
carousel...

850
01:23:01,750 --> 01:23:03,948
and a seesaw.

851
01:23:04,189 --> 01:23:06,390
They all move motorised, of course.

852
01:23:06,630 --> 01:23:09,069
People said they saw the fleas.
I can see the fleas.

853
01:23:09,309 --> 01:23:11,470
Can't you see the fleas?

854
01:23:11,710 --> 01:23:17,310
Clown fleas, high-wire fleas,
and fleas on parade.

855
01:23:21,789 --> 01:23:24,590
With this place...

856
01:23:25,229 --> 01:23:29,189
I wanted to show them something
that wasn't an illusion.

857
01:23:29,430 --> 01:23:33,909
Something that was real.
Something that they could...

858
01:23:35,430 --> 01:23:38,310
see and touch.

859
01:23:41,390 --> 01:23:45,189
An aim not devoid of merit.

860
01:23:48,029 --> 01:23:52,989
But you can't think through this one, John.
You have to feel it.

861
01:23:53,631 --> 01:23:56,791
You're absolutely right.

862
01:23:57,030 --> 01:23:59,109
Hiring Nedry was a mistake,
that's obvious.

863
01:23:59,350 --> 01:24:01,791
We're overdependent on automation,
I can see that now.

864
01:24:02,030 --> 01:24:05,070
Next time, everything's correctable.

865
01:24:05,310 --> 01:24:09,871
Creation is an act of sheer will.

866
01:24:10,630 --> 01:24:12,829
Next time, it'll be flawless.

867
01:24:13,070 --> 01:24:16,149
It's still the flea circus. It's all an illusion.

868
01:24:16,390 --> 01:24:17,670
When we have control—

869
01:24:17,909 --> 01:24:20,390
You've never had control!
That's the illusion.

870
01:24:20,630 --> 01:24:24,310
I was overwhelmed
by the power of this place.

871
01:24:24,550 --> 01:24:25,789
I made a mistake, too.

872
01:24:26,029 --> 01:24:31,149
I didn't have enough respect
for that power, and it's out now.

873
01:24:32,190 --> 01:24:36,711
The only thing that matters now
are the people we love.

874
01:24:37,310 --> 01:24:40,831
Alan, Lex, and Tim.

875
01:24:40,911 --> 01:24:45,390
John, they're out there,
where people are dying.

876
01:25:00,270 --> 01:25:02,831
It's good.

877
01:25:05,149 --> 01:25:08,029
Spared no expense.

878
01:25:32,789 --> 01:25:36,229
- Go away!
- It's okay. It's a Brachiosaurus.

879
01:25:36,470 --> 01:25:39,310
It's a "Veggie-saurus", Lex.
"Veggie-saurus."

880
01:25:39,550 --> 01:25:41,350
Veggie.

881
01:25:41,590 --> 01:25:44,791
Come on. Come on, girl.

882
01:25:47,350 --> 01:25:50,152
Come on, baby.

883
01:25:54,832 --> 01:25:57,751
I'm not letting go.

884
01:26:07,711 --> 01:26:10,551
- It looks like it has a cold.
- Yeah. Maybe.

885
01:26:10,791 --> 01:26:12,430
- Can I touch it?
- Sure.

886
01:26:12,671 --> 01:26:17,070
Just think of it as kind of a big cow.

887
01:26:18,831 --> 01:26:21,470
I like cows.

888
01:26:29,631 --> 01:26:33,351
Come on, girl. Come on up here, girl.

889
01:26:39,030 --> 01:26:41,711
God bless you.

890
01:26:46,350 --> 01:26:49,430
Great. Now she'll never try anything new.

891
01:26:49,671 --> 01:26:52,430
She'll sit in her room, never come out,
play on her computer.

892
01:26:52,671 --> 01:26:55,190
- I'm a hacker.
- That's what I said. You're a nerd.

893
01:26:55,430 --> 01:26:58,510
I am not a computer nerd.
I prefer to be called a hacker.

894
01:26:58,751 --> 01:27:01,310
God.

895
01:27:04,591 --> 01:27:07,631
You know what this is?

896
01:27:07,990 --> 01:27:10,350
It's a dinosaur egg.

897
01:27:10,591 --> 01:27:15,150
The dinosaurs are breeding.

898
01:27:16,712 --> 01:27:21,711
But my grandpa said
all the dinosaurs were girls.

899
01:27:21,952 --> 01:27:25,591
- Amphibian DNA.
- What's that?

900
01:27:27,831 --> 01:27:32,511
The film said they used frog DNA
to fill in the gene-sequence gaps.

901
01:27:32,751 --> 01:27:36,671
They mutated the dinosaur genetic code.

902
01:27:36,911 --> 01:27:40,551
And blended it with that of frogs.

903
01:27:41,871 --> 01:27:45,992
Some West African frogs have been known
to change sex from male to female...

904
01:27:46,230 --> 01:27:49,671
in a single sex environment.

905
01:27:50,110 --> 01:27:52,711
Malcolm was right.

906
01:27:52,951 --> 01:27:55,430
Look.

907
01:28:02,350 --> 01:28:05,470
Life found a way.

908
01:28:07,192 --> 01:28:11,032
That's crazy. You're out of your mind.
He's absolutely out of his mind.

909
01:28:11,270 --> 01:28:13,230
Wait. What exactly would this mean?

910
01:28:13,470 --> 01:28:17,030
We're talking about a calculated risk,
which is the only option left to us.

911
01:28:17,270 --> 01:28:21,791
We'll never find the command Nedry used.
He's covered his tracks far too well.

912
01:28:22,029 --> 01:28:24,591
I think it's obvious now
that he's not coming back.

913
01:28:24,832 --> 01:28:29,030
- So, shutting down the entire system—
- Get somebody else. I won't do it.

914
01:28:29,271 --> 01:28:35,070
Shutting down the system is the only way
to wipe out everything he did.

915
01:28:35,311 --> 01:28:38,671
Now, as I understand it,
all the systems will then come back...

916
01:28:38,911 --> 01:28:41,791
on their original start-up mode. Correct?

917
01:28:42,032 --> 01:28:44,752
Theoretically, yes.
We've never shut down before.

918
01:28:44,990 --> 01:28:47,711
- It may not come back on.
- Would we get the phones back?

919
01:28:47,952 --> 01:28:49,872
Yes, again, in theory.

920
01:28:50,112 --> 01:28:53,230
What about the lysine contingency?
We could put that into effect.

921
01:28:53,470 --> 01:28:56,032
- What's that?
- That's absolutely out of the question.

922
01:28:56,270 --> 01:28:58,831
The lysine contingency
prevents the spread of animals...

923
01:28:59,070 --> 01:29:00,711
if they ever get off the island.

924
01:29:00,951 --> 01:29:04,311
Wu inserted a gene that creates
a faulty enzyme in protein metabolism.

925
01:29:04,551 --> 01:29:07,192
The animals can't manufacture
the amino acid, lysine.

926
01:29:07,430 --> 01:29:10,871
Unless they're supplied with lysine by us,
they slip into a coma and die.

927
01:29:11,112 --> 01:29:14,952
People are dying.

928
01:29:17,511 --> 01:29:21,271
Will you please shut down the system?

929
01:29:40,911 --> 01:29:43,992
Hold on to your butts.

930
01:30:01,591 --> 01:30:02,551
It's okay.

931
01:30:02,792 --> 01:30:06,831
Look, see that? It's on. It worked.

932
01:30:07,752 --> 01:30:09,871
What do you mean it worked?
Everything's off.

933
01:30:10,112 --> 01:30:13,912
Shutdown tripped the circuit breakers.
Turn them back on, reboot a few systems:

934
01:30:14,150 --> 01:30:17,231
Telephones, security doors,
half dozen others, but it worked.

935
01:30:17,471 --> 01:30:19,631
- System's ready.
- Where are the breakers?

936
01:30:19,871 --> 01:30:21,353
The shed at end of the compound.

937
01:30:21,592 --> 01:30:24,073
Three minutes, I'll have the power back on
in the park.

938
01:30:24,311 --> 01:30:27,113
Just to be safe, I want everybody
in the emergency bunker...

939
01:30:27,351 --> 01:30:30,591
until Mr. Arnold returns,
and the whole system's running again.

940
01:30:30,832 --> 01:30:33,591
- I'm tired. I'm hot.
- I'm dirty.

941
01:30:33,832 --> 01:30:37,832
Looking at the map,
I'd say the visitor's centre is just a mile...

942
01:30:38,072 --> 01:30:41,192
over that rise there.

943
01:30:43,992 --> 01:30:46,470
What is that?

944
01:30:47,631 --> 01:30:51,231
Tim, can you tell me what they are?

945
01:30:56,511 --> 01:30:57,551
Gallimimus.

946
01:30:57,792 --> 01:31:01,672
Are those meat-eating?

947
01:31:01,752 --> 01:31:04,471
"Meat-asauruses"?

948
01:31:07,792 --> 01:31:09,992
Look at the wheeling.
Uniform direction changes.

949
01:31:10,231 --> 01:31:14,033
Just like a flock of birds
evading a predator.

950
01:31:14,271 --> 01:31:18,752
They're flocking this way.

951
01:31:58,472 --> 01:32:00,431
- I want to go now.
- Just look how it eats.

952
01:32:00,671 --> 01:32:01,752
Please.

953
01:32:01,992 --> 01:32:04,471
I bet you'll never look at birds
the same way again.

954
01:32:04,712 --> 01:32:08,192
- Yes.
- Go now.

955
01:32:08,471 --> 01:32:12,511
Okay. Keep low and follow me.

956
01:32:13,351 --> 01:32:16,552
Look how much blood.

957
01:32:17,193 --> 01:32:19,832
God. Something went wrong.

958
01:32:20,073 --> 01:32:24,952
Something's happened.
Something's wrong.

959
01:32:26,072 --> 01:32:27,431
This is just a delay.

960
01:32:27,672 --> 01:32:30,631
That's all it is.
All major theme parks have delays.

961
01:32:30,872 --> 01:32:35,193
When they opened Disneyland in 1956,
nothing worked.

962
01:32:35,431 --> 01:32:38,832
If the Pirates of the Caribbean
breaks down, the pirates don't eat tourists.

963
01:32:39,073 --> 01:32:43,792
I can't wait any more. Something's wrong.
I'm gonna go get the power back on.

964
01:32:44,032 --> 01:32:46,912
You can't just stroll down the road,
you know.

965
01:32:47,153 --> 01:32:51,792
Don't let's be too hasty.
I mean, he's only been gone...

966
01:32:52,872 --> 01:32:56,313
- I'm going with you.
- Okay.

967
01:33:09,832 --> 01:33:12,311
Sorry.

968
01:33:14,672 --> 01:33:18,631
Now, this isn't going to be just like
switching on the kitchen light.

969
01:33:18,872 --> 01:33:23,591
But I think I can follow this
and then talk you through it.

970
01:33:26,512 --> 01:33:28,952
Right.

971
01:33:33,271 --> 01:33:35,073
It ought to be me, really, going.

972
01:33:35,313 --> 01:33:39,233
- Why?
- I'm a... And you're a...

973
01:33:39,472 --> 01:33:41,552
- Look.
- Come on, let's go.

974
01:33:41,792 --> 01:33:44,752
We can discuss sexism
in survival situations when I get back.

975
01:33:44,993 --> 01:33:49,953
You just take me through this step by step.
I'm on Channel 2.

976
01:34:23,953 --> 01:34:26,953
The shutdown must've turned off
all the fences.

977
01:34:27,193 --> 01:34:32,193
Damn it, even Nedry knew better
than to mess with the Raptor pen.

978
01:34:36,432 --> 01:34:39,033
Come on, this way.

979
01:34:44,752 --> 01:34:46,592
I can see the shed from here.

980
01:34:46,832 --> 01:34:48,591
We can make it if we run.

981
01:34:48,832 --> 01:34:51,912
No. We can't.

982
01:34:52,153 --> 01:34:53,632
Why not?

983
01:34:53,873 --> 01:34:56,792
Because we're being hunted.

984
01:34:57,033 --> 01:35:00,432
In the bushes, straight ahead.

985
01:35:01,472 --> 01:35:03,033
It's all right.

986
01:35:03,273 --> 01:35:05,632
Like hell it is.

987
01:35:05,872 --> 01:35:10,393
Run. Towards the shed.

988
01:35:11,033 --> 01:35:13,112
I've got her.

989
01:35:13,353 --> 01:35:16,271
Go. Now!

990
01:35:33,193 --> 01:35:35,993
Mr. Arnold?

991
01:35:39,632 --> 01:35:41,153
John, I'm in.

992
01:35:41,393 --> 01:35:43,792
Great.

993
01:35:43,792 --> 01:35:47,912
Ahead of you is a metal staircase.
Go down it.

994
01:35:48,153 --> 01:35:50,993
Okay. Going down.

995
01:35:51,234 --> 01:35:54,832
After 20 or 30 feet
you come to a T-junction. Take a left.

996
01:35:55,073 --> 01:35:59,552
- Have her follow the main cable.
- I know how to read a schematic.

997
01:36:26,993 --> 01:36:30,594
I guess that means the power's off.

998
01:36:41,832 --> 01:36:45,832
- That's not funny.
- That was great.

999
01:36:46,752 --> 01:36:50,073
Well, maybe we, we could...

1000
01:37:05,713 --> 01:37:08,233
Damn it. Dead end.

1001
01:37:08,472 --> 01:37:11,153
Wait a minute.

1002
01:37:11,153 --> 01:37:14,993
There should have been a right turn
back there somewhere.

1003
01:37:15,233 --> 01:37:17,354
Look above you.

1004
01:37:17,592 --> 01:37:21,153
There should be a bundle of cable and
pipes all heading in the same direction.

1005
01:37:21,393 --> 01:37:22,912
Follow that.

1006
01:37:23,153 --> 01:37:26,713
Okay. Following the piping.

1007
01:37:27,793 --> 01:37:31,913
I bet I could climb over to the other side
before you could even get to the top.

1008
01:37:32,154 --> 01:37:35,073
- What would you give me?
- Respect.

1009
01:37:35,314 --> 01:37:38,753
Come on, guys, it's not a race.

1010
01:37:38,873 --> 01:37:41,552
I see the box.

1011
01:37:41,753 --> 01:37:44,873
It says, "High Voltage."

1012
01:37:47,592 --> 01:37:50,033
Okay.

1013
01:37:52,474 --> 01:37:55,432
Good. Here we go.

1014
01:37:55,672 --> 01:37:57,193
Over the top.

1015
01:37:57,432 --> 01:37:59,712
Take your time.

1016
01:37:59,953 --> 01:38:01,394
Good. Come on.

1017
01:38:01,632 --> 01:38:04,953
Find your footing.

1018
01:38:05,634 --> 01:38:08,073
You can't throw the main switch by hand.

1019
01:38:08,314 --> 01:38:11,434
You've got to pump up the primer handle
in order to get the charge.

1020
01:38:11,673 --> 01:38:14,434
It's large, flat and grey.

1021
01:38:14,673 --> 01:38:17,713
All right, here I go. Okay?

1022
01:38:17,953 --> 01:38:23,073
One, two, three, four.

1023
01:38:23,314 --> 01:38:25,314
Okay, charged.

1024
01:38:25,552 --> 01:38:29,234
Under the words "Contact Position,"
there's a round green button...

1025
01:38:29,474 --> 01:38:31,993
which says "Push to Close."

1026
01:38:32,234 --> 01:38:35,472
Push to close.

1027
01:38:37,353 --> 01:38:39,793
Push it.

1028
01:38:52,913 --> 01:38:57,314
The red buttons turn on
the individual park systems.

1029
01:38:57,554 --> 01:39:00,154
Switch them on.

1030
01:39:09,833 --> 01:39:11,792
- Get down now!
- You're gonna have to jump.

1031
01:39:12,033 --> 01:39:13,634
You crazy? I'm not gonna jump.

1032
01:39:13,872 --> 01:39:16,234
- Do what Dr. Grant says!
- Let go. I'll count to three.

1033
01:39:16,472 --> 01:39:20,274
One, two, three!

1034
01:39:22,715 --> 01:39:24,234
I'm coming up to get you.

1035
01:39:24,475 --> 01:39:26,073
I've got to get Tim.

1036
01:39:26,314 --> 01:39:29,474
Okay. I'm gonna count to three.

1037
01:39:30,474 --> 01:39:32,913
One...

1038
01:39:33,314 --> 01:39:35,833
two...

1039
01:39:44,993 --> 01:39:47,594
He's not breathing.

1040
01:39:51,153 --> 01:39:54,913
I think we're back in business!

1041
01:41:29,154 --> 01:41:31,833
Clever girl.

1042
01:41:53,673 --> 01:41:56,154
No, Tim!

1043
01:41:57,555 --> 01:42:00,835
Good boy.

1044
01:42:03,755 --> 01:42:06,394
Three.

1045
01:42:09,875 --> 01:42:12,395
Hello?

1046
01:42:22,274 --> 01:42:24,755
I'm gonna have to find the others.

1047
01:42:24,994 --> 01:42:29,114
Get you to a doctor.

1048
01:42:29,594 --> 01:42:32,793
- Lex, you look after Tim.
- Yeah.

1049
01:42:33,033 --> 01:42:36,395
Your hair is all sticking up.

1050
01:42:36,835 --> 01:42:40,515
Big Tim, the human piece of toast.

1051
01:42:43,154 --> 01:42:46,715
I'll be back soon. I promise.

1052
01:43:11,434 --> 01:43:13,873
Run.

1053
01:44:25,873 --> 01:44:28,913
Just the two Raptors, right?

1054
01:44:29,154 --> 01:44:30,795
You sure the third one's contained?

1055
01:44:31,034 --> 01:44:35,956
Yes, unless they figure out
how to open doors.

1056
01:44:59,994 --> 01:45:04,515
- What is it?
- It's a Velociraptor.

1057
01:45:05,715 --> 01:45:08,635
It's inside.

1058
01:45:31,676 --> 01:45:34,194
Follow me.

1059
01:48:01,916 --> 01:48:03,716
It's in there.

1060
01:48:03,956 --> 01:48:06,636
Control room.

1061
01:48:11,234 --> 01:48:15,876
- We can call for help?
- We've got to reboot the system first.

1062
01:48:17,115 --> 01:48:18,996
The door locks!

1063
01:48:19,234 --> 01:48:22,675
Ellie, boot up the door locks!

1064
01:48:30,315 --> 01:48:32,836
No, boot up the door locks!

1065
01:48:33,076 --> 01:48:38,237
- Get back! Boot up the door locks.
- You can't hold it by yourself!

1066
01:48:44,837 --> 01:48:48,676
It's a UNIX system. I know this.

1067
01:48:48,916 --> 01:48:50,756
It's all the files of the whole park.

1068
01:48:50,996 --> 01:48:54,595
It tells you everything.
I've got to find the right file.

1069
01:48:54,836 --> 01:48:57,836
Try to reach the gun.

1070
01:49:04,516 --> 01:49:07,877
I can't get it unless I move.

1071
01:49:21,555 --> 01:49:25,515
This is it. This might be the right file.

1072
01:49:39,036 --> 01:49:42,036
- You got it!
- Yes!

1073
01:49:50,836 --> 01:49:51,676
What works?

1074
01:49:51,916 --> 01:49:55,997
All security systems are enabled.
We got it!

1075
01:50:03,996 --> 01:50:04,996
Grant.

1076
01:50:05,235 --> 01:50:08,275
Mr. Hammond, the phones are working.

1077
01:50:08,515 --> 01:50:11,355
- The children all right?
- The children are fine.

1078
01:50:11,595 --> 01:50:15,756
Call the mainland.
Tell 'em to send the damn helicopters.

1079
01:50:15,996 --> 01:50:19,036
It's gonna cut through the glass!

1080
01:51:09,917 --> 01:51:12,237
Move!

1081
01:53:09,118 --> 01:53:13,756
After careful consideration,
I've decided not to endorse your park.

1082
01:53:13,997 --> 01:53:16,636
So have I.